+<!-- Reaper_Tor.asp - VPN > Tor (Reaper-native UI, VPN wave, 2026-10-23).
+     Replaces Advanced_TOR_Content.asp (parity file build-scripts/tests/ui_parity/Advanced_TOR_Content.json).
+     Keys: Tor_enable, Tor_socksport, Tor_transport, Tor_dnsport (rc/services.c start_Tor_proxy writes /tmp/torrc),
+     Tor_redir_list (rc/firewall.c nat_setting / nat_setting2 REDIRECT rules and the FORWARD DROP of everything that is
+     not TCP from the selected devices; empty list = every LAN device). Apply posts only the changed keys with
+     restart_tor (rc/services.c: stop/start_Tor_proxy + start_firewall).
+     Every stock check is kept (ports numeric 0-65535 -> made 1-65535 and distinct, MAC format, duplicate device).
+     Deliberate differences from stock:
+     - the scope select (whole LAN / selected devices) with an EMPTY device list no longer saves an empty list: stock
+       posted Tor_redir_list= which rc reads as "every LAN device" (inventory B-TOR-2). It is refused.
+     - ports: empty, 0 and duplicate ports refused (an empty port leaves `SocksPort ` in torrc and Tor will not start;
+       REDIRECT --to-ports 0 is invalid; two listeners on one port fail).
+     - Tor_redir_list is not on the libnvram JFFS whitelist, so the 1 KB nvram tuple wall applies: the device list is
+       capped at 991 bytes (about 55 MAC entries) and the budget is shown; stock had no cap and the save failed silently.
+     - turning Tor on, or widening the scope to the whole LAN, asks first: the selected devices' TCP and DNS go through Tor
+       and ALL their other traffic (UDP, ping) is dropped, and Tor needs minutes to bootstrap.
+     - the list is read through nvram_char_to_ascii and every entry is escaped; entries rc also accepts (an IPv4 address or
+       range) are shown and kept if present, but the editor adds MAC entries only, as stock did (IPv6 of an address-selected
