+<!-- Reaper_VPNDirector.asp - VPN > VPN Director (Reaper-native UI, VPN wave, 2026-10-23).
+     Replaces Advanced_VPNDirector.asp (parity file build-scripts/tests/ui_parity/Advanced_VPNDirector.json).
+     One setting: vpndirector_rulelist, record <enable>description>local>remote>interface (libovpn/amvpn_routing.c
+     _write_routing_rules: enable atoi, target WAN -> table main, OVPNn -> ovpncN, WGCn -> wgcN; "" or 0.0.0.0 = any).
+     On this build the list is NOT nvram: httpd serves it from /jffs/openvpn/vpndirector_rulelist (web.c ej_nvram_char_to_ascii
+     special case -> amvpn_get_policy_rules, 8000-byte buffer) and stores it with amvpn_set_policy_rules (web.c apply path).
+     Apply posts the list only when a rule was edited, with restart_vpnrouting0 (rc/services.c vpnrouting dispatch, unit 0:
+     WAN rules + every WireGuard and OpenVPN client's rules, killswitches, exclusive DNS, then reaper_pbr_regen).
+     Rows nobody edited are written back byte for byte. Every stock check is kept: description without < > ' % (32 chars),
+     local / remote empty or IPv4 or IPv4/CIDR, 199 rules, 7999 characters.
+     Deliberate differences from stock:
+     - the client enable / disable toggles and the Edit jumps are not here: they saved vpn_clientx_eas / wgcN_enable at once
+       (httpApi.nvramSet + rc_service) from a rules page. Each client's state is shown read-only with a link to the VPN
+       Client page, which owns those keys.
+     - a duplicate rule (same interface, local and remote) is refused; a rule matching every address asks first (it also
+       catches the router's own traffic); a rule to a client that is switched off asks first (its Killswitch decides
+       whether matched devices are blocked or use the WAN).
+     - the stored order is kept (stock re-sorted the list by interface on every Apply); the table groups by interface for
+       display only. Priority inside one table does not depend on order (every rule of a group routes to the same table).
+     - descriptions and addresses are escaped; a record rc would skip (not five fields) is shown dimmed and can be deleted.
+     Policy Routing (Reaper_VPNRouting.asp) is a different feature that stays: it selects traffic by firewall object,
+     domain set or device and marks it (fwmark 0xN0000, ip rule pref 9000-9199), so its rules win over VPN Director's
+     address rules (pref 10000+) for the flows they name; both follow each client's Killswitch. -->
